CS 250 – Journal 8
10/19/2025	

Throughout the Computer Science program, the portfolio acts as a professional collection of work that demonstrates growth, skill development, and real-world application of programming concepts. In this course, I’ve continued developing my understanding of Agile and Scrum methodologies while applying them through hands-on assignments and team-based collaboration. Reflecting on this experience helps solidify the connection between theory and practice, providing long-term context for how Agile principles guide effective software development.
The artifact I’m submitting for this course is my Sprint Review and Retrospective document from the final project. This deliverable showcases the Agile and Scrum practices I’ve applied across the course, including sprint planning, backlog prioritization, team communication, and process improvement.
How do I interpret user needs and implement them into a program? How does creating “user stories” help with this?
Interpreting user needs requires active listening, clear communication, and translating customer goals into technical functionality. Creating user stories plays a vital role in this process because it defines requirements from the user’s perspective - focusing on the value the feature provides rather than just technical specifications. For example, writing a story in the format “As a user, I want this feature, so that we achieve this benefit.” ensures the team understands both what to build and why it matters. During this course, I learned that refining these stories during backlog grooming sessions helps eliminate ambiguity and keeps development aligned with stakeholder expectations.
How do I approach developing programs? What Agile processes do I hope to incorporate into my future development work?
I approach development as an iterative and collaborative process guided by continuous improvement. Agile frameworks have taught me to break large projects into smaller, manageable increments, each delivering tangible value to the user. In my future work, I hope to integrate Agile practices such as Sprint Planning, Daily Standups, Sprint Reviews, and Retrospectives to maintain transparency and adaptability. These processes ensure that teams stay focused on the highest-priority tasks while remaining flexible to respond to feedback and change. By emphasizing incremental progress and constant reflection, development becomes more efficient and less error-prone.
What does it mean to be a good team member in software development?
Being a good team member means balancing technical contribution with strong communication and collaboration. A good developer not only writes efficient code but also contributes to a supportive team environment where ideas can be shared freely. Throughout CS 250, I learned that effective teamwork involves accountability, empathy, and a commitment to shared goals. In Agile environments, success depends on trust. Each member must be reliable, transparent, and willing to assist others when challenges arise. Practicing these behaviors ensures that the team delivers quality work consistently while maintaining a positive and productive atmosphere.

